[{:adr/supersedes "", :adr/superseded-by "", :adr/related ["ADR-2608124800" "ADR-2608125000" "ADR-2608130600" "ADR-2607289600" "ADR-2607198300"], :adr/status "accepted", :adr/id "ADR-2608131800", :adr/body "## What was measured\n\n`scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs` landed on 2026-08-12 as a home for the\ndetectors that structurally cannot be murakumo fleet gates: a gate ships only\nthe target repo's own tree, so a detector reading `orgs/` scans an empty\nworkspace on a node. `root-permit-index` failed **298 times across 259 distinct\ntips** for exactly that, reporting the wrong invariant while doing it.\n\nThe tick's own stated growth path is that **the next detector should be a\nregistry entry, not a sixth uninstalled plist**. Measured 2026-08-13:\n**13 scripts under `scripts/verify-*.cljs` mention `orgs/`. Exactly 2 were\nregistered.** This is the sweep of the other 11.\n\n**Four were registered. Two of the nine ruled out do not read `orgs/` at all** —\nthe grep that produced the list of 13 matched a usage comment and a regex\nliteral, not a code path. That is worth stating plainly: the list a sweep starts\nfrom is itself a measurement, and this one was 15% wrong in the direction of\novercounting.\n\n## Registered — four, each with its runtime measured on this machine\n\nEvery number below was produced by running the detector here, not copied from a\nneighbour. **The machine's load is part of the measurement.** During this\nsession `sysctl -n vm.loadavg` read between 1 and 187 on 10 cores, and one\ndetector's wall time moved by 3.7x with no change to its input. Timeouts are\nsized against the loaded numbers.\n\n### `verify-layer-deps` — 6h tick, 15m timeout\n\n`manifest/layers.edn` declares a dependency DAG over the workspace's layers and\nthis script is the only thing that checks it. ADR-2607289600 measured the state\nit guards against: the layer plane is definition-only — west.yml has zero\n`layer-*` groups and `gen-west-manifest.cljs` does not read `layers.edn` — so a\nviolating `:local/root` edge is caught by **nothing else in the pipeline**. It\nhas already found real breakage: the Phase-1 dedup worklist\n(`90-docs/2607171100-phase1-dedup-worklist.csv`, row 210) records\n`cloud-itonami-isic-855` with a `:local/root` one directory too high, credited\nto this script.\n\nMeasured twice, and the spread is the finding: **26.5s at load ~1, 2m06s at\nload 133** for identical work, of which only 10.1s was user CPU. Both runs:\n4,415 repo directories under `orgs/`, 3,489 `deps.edn` parsed, 2,978\n`:local/root` edges, **7 violations**, all `:internal-numbered-dir` in three app\nrepos. exit 0 with `--report`, exit 1 without.\n\n### `verify-kotoba-host-parity` — 6h tick, 5m timeout\n\nThree repos hold three copies of one fact — which host imports a `.kotoba` guest\nmay call: `kotoba-lang/lang/host-parity.edn`, kototama's `contract.cljc`, and\nwasm-webcomponent's `actor-host.js`. Only this script compares them.\n\n**It was fail-fast, and that hid half of what it knows.** Every check called\n`process.exit(1)` on the spot, so a run only ever showed the first violation.\nThat is precisely the defect ADR-2608124800 measured on `root-repository-roles`,\nwhere a fail-fast verifier meant every later assertion had gone unexecuted since\n2026-08-03 with nobody able to see it. The checks here are independent — none\nconsumes a value another produces — so they now all run and the failures are\ncollected. The exit code is unchanged.\n\n**Converting it immediately produced a second finding that had never been\nvisible**: alongside `import-sets-drifted`, the browser matrix disagrees with\nthe implemented host functions on 17 ids (`:http-get` in the matrix and not\nimplemented; sixteen `pg-*`/`json-*`/`cbor-*` implemented and not in the\nmatrix). Measured at load ~133: 11.7s wall, 0.77s user CPU, **59 declared host\nimports across 3 files, exit 1, 2 findings.**\n\n### `verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover` — 6h tick, 5m timeout\n\nADR-2607198300 makes it repo-wide that a shipped artefact does not depend on a\nJVM, Node or Rust host, and\n`90-docs/migration/kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover.edn` is the frozen list of\nallowed exceptions. Nothing else compares that list against the four `deps.edn`\nfiles it freezes, and those files are under `orgs/`.\n\nMeasured at load ~133: 6.0s wall, 0.82s user CPU, **10 contract/dependency paths\nresolved, exit 1, 1 finding** — `orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/deps.edn` carries a\nJVM/runtime dependency the inventory does not cover.\n\n### `verify-tsukuru-factory-plane` — 24h tick, 90m timeout\n\nThree factory datasets that differ **by what each company consented to**, not by\nhow real they are. Every way that distinction breaks is silent: a tag collapses,\nrows drop, a `did:web` leaks into the unconsented set, and Datalog keeps\nanswering. The largest lie available here is an un-onboarded company appearing\nas registered.\n\nTwo things decided this one. First, **it was itself the failure it guards\nagainst** until 2026-08-13: an empty plane query made it skip all three checks\nand exit 0 — `edn/read-string` on empty input returns nil without throwing, and\nnil meant *skip* rather than *fail* (ADR-2608130600). That is the same shape as\nthe five retired `security-gate-*` scripts, living inside the verifier this\nworkspace built to recover from them. Second, **its only scheduled caller is\n`scripts/tsukuru-maturity-tick.cljs`**, driven by `com.gftd.tsukuru-maturity-tick`\n— one of the five plists ADR-2608124800 found had never been installed.\nRegistering it here gives it a home **without** installing that plist, which\nalso launches an autonomous model loop that commits and pushes. The ADR is\nexplicit that those are different decisions.\n\nIts own header asks for exactly this: *6 時間 tick に載せる前に、そのホストの\nload で測り直す*. Done, twice. **Standalone at load 157–187: 18m21s wall, 1093.4s\nof it the single plane load plus three queries, 256s user CPU. Through the tick\nitself at load ~110–157: 822s, exit 0, scanned=2221, 0 findings.** The dataset\ndetail: candidates 2,119 / registry-seed 99 / seed 3, each above its floor;\nthree datasets disjoint; 0 `did:web` in candidates; 0 fabricated\n`:company/lei`. The header records ~295s on a quiet host, so **the load term\ndominates by up to 3.7x** — which is the whole reason it asked. 24h rather than\n6h for the reason `verify-require-graph` already carries: 256s of CPU four times\na day on a machine at load 157 is a real cost, and a consent-tier partition\nchanges on human timescales.\n\n## Ruled out — nine, each under a stated class\n\nThe registry header already ruled out three classes. Two more were needed, and\ntwo entries turned out not to belong to the sweep at all.\n\n| script | class | why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `verify-duplicate-registrations` | **needs `gh`** | calls `gh api repos/<slug>` once per candidate group to confirm two paths are one upstream by GitHub id; rate-limited, one token shared with every concurrent session |\n| `verify-namespace-collisions` | **not swept** | a sibling session was working on it on 2026-08-13; registering it concurrently would have pinned an argv being changed underneath |\n| `verify-repository-roles` | **already hosted** | it is the fleet gate `root-repository-roles`, diagnosed and returned to green by ADR-2608124800 |\n| `verify-parity-probes` | **already hosted, and does not read `orgs/`** | it is `root-parity-probe-policy`; `orgs/` appears only inside a regex matching the TEXT of a probe. Its input is `90-docs/maturity/engine-parity.datoms.edn`, in root's own tree |\n| `verify-no-babashka` | **does not read `orgs/`** | `--root orgs` is a usage comment; the default root is `.`. ADR-2608124800 already classes it root-tree-only and gatable as-is, so what it lacks is a `gates.edn` line |\n| `verify-clj-everywhere` | **not a detector** | a test-suite orchestrator: four audits then `clojure -M:test` in five repos. No per-finding output to key on, resolves Maven over HTTPS, writes `~/.m2` — `:writes :none` would be false |\n| `verify-kotoba-pages` | **needs a real browser** | drives Playwright against `file://` pages; CLAUDE.md's resource governor caps concurrent browser probes and this machine's parallel sessions already contend for OS focus |\n| `verify-vendored-copies` | already registered | 2026-08-12 |\n| `verify-require-graph` | already registered | 2026-08-12 |\n\n**`verify-no-babashka` was measured anyway**, because pointing it at `orgs/` was\nthe obvious thing to try: `--root orgs --allow-zero` ran for **roughly 38\nminutes without emitting a line** and was killed. It reads **every file in the\ntree in full** to test for a `bb` shebang, so its cost is dominated by the size\nof the artefacts it is not looking for. That is a defect in the detector rather\nthan a property of the question, and it is recorded here so the next person does\nnot rediscover it by waiting.\n\n## The protocol each registered detector was taught\n\nEach now supports `--findings`, emitting the same protocol the two existing\nentries use — read out of their implementations rather than invented:\n\n    SCANNED<TAB>n<TAB>unit                    one evidence line, n > 0\n    FINDING<TAB>severity<TAB>key<TAB>detail   one line per finding\n\nWithout the flag, output is byte-identical to before in three of the four; the\nexception is `verify-kotoba-host-parity`, where the fail-fast-to-collect change\nalters the unflagged report too, by showing more. Exit codes are unchanged\neverywhere.\n\n**The keys are structural on purpose.** A key that embeds a count — `7\nviolations` — would change while the defect stayed the same, and the tick would\nreport the identical violation as resolved-and-new on every run, destroying the\none thing the design exists for: telling a red that appeared today from one that\nhas been true for weeks. So keys are `internal-numbered-dir:<repo>:<dir>`,\n`jvm-dep-not-frozen:<path>`, `import-sets-drifted`, `dataset-count:<dataset>`.\n\n**The evidence count must be something the run resolved, not a constant.**\n`verify-layer-deps` counts repo directories actually walked, `host-parity`\ncounts imports resolved out of the three files, `cutover` counts paths it\nopened, `tsukuru` counts factory entities the plane returned. Each of those is 0\nin exactly the situation the floor exists to catch — a detector that started and\ncould not read its inputs.\n\n## Both floors were watched firing\n\nA floor nobody has watched fire is not a floor. Both were broken deliberately in\nthrowaway copies, and in both cases the red had to be caused by the intended\ncheck rather than by something else tripping first.\n\n**Admission floor.** `:writes` removed from one entry in a copy of the registry:\n\n    ADMISSION FAILED — refusing to run any detector:\n      :verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover: :writes must be :none -- this home never writes to a checkout\n    EXIT=2\n\nExactly one problem is reported, naming the entry and the field — no other\npredicate fired, so the exit 2 is attributable. The real registry was never\nedited; the break was in `/tmp`.\n\n**Evidence floor.** A copy of `verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover` with exactly\none line deleted — the `SCANNED` `println`, confirmed by `diff` as a single-line\nchange — run beside an unmodified copy of the same file in the same tick:\n\n    running evidence-control …\n    evidence-control → ok exit 1 3s 1 finding(s) scanned=10\n      NEW       jvm-dep-not-frozen:orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/deps.edn  —  JVM/runtime dependency is not in the frozen legacy inventory\n    running evidence-broken …\n    evidence-broken → inconclusive exit 1 2s 1 finding(s) scanned= no evidence line matching /SCANNED\\t[1-9][0-9]*\\t/ -- the run cannot be recorded as clean\n\n**The two runs are the same detector, the same exit code, and the same emitted\nfinding.** The only difference is the evidence line, and that alone moves the\nrecorded status from `:ok` to `:inconclusive`. The state file records\n`:last-status :ok` with `:last-scanned 10` for the control and\n`:last-status :inconclusive` for the broken copy — and, as designed, the\ninconclusive run does not overwrite the finding set, so it cannot report a\nstanding red as resolved.\n\n## The live tick still runs\n\n    running verify-layer-deps …\n    verify-layer-deps → ok exit 0 37s 7 finding(s) scanned=4415\n    running verify-kotoba-host-parity …\n    verify-kotoba-host-parity → ok exit 1 3s 2 finding(s) scanned=59\n    running verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover …\n    verify-kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover → ok exit 1 3s 1 finding(s) scanned=10\n    state → ~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/state.edn\n\nand separately, because it is the long one:\n\n    running verify-tsukuru-factory-plane …\n    verify-tsukuru-factory-plane → ok exit 0 822s 0 finding(s) scanned=2221\n\nAll six entries were admitted (admission runs over the whole registry before\n`--only` narrows it). Each new detector's first run is a BASELINE, which is why\nten findings appear as NEW at once and why the tick reports it as a first\nmeasurement rather than as ten new alarms.\n\n## The ADR validator was watched firing too\n\nThis document is gated on a validator that asserts one entity, every key a\nkeyword, and a body ending in the exact words written — run in the same shell\nexpression as the commit, because running validation and commit as separate\nsteps is how the truncated ADR landed. That validator was itself demonstrated\nagainst the documented failure shape, a file that **still parses as exactly one\nentity**:\n\n    ADR VALIDATION FAILED: non-keyword key stray-remainder -- this is what a truncating quote looks like\n    EXIT=1\n\n**Two things went wrong while building it, and both are the kind that pass\nreview.** First, its first version read positional arguments with\n`(drop 2 js/process.argv)` — the idiom the detectors in this tree use for\nflags — and under nbb `argv[2]` is the **script path**, so the validator\ncheerfully parsed *itself* and reported `top level is not a vector`. A validator\nthat validates the wrong file is a floor that cannot fire. Second, the first\nattempt to break the real document injected a quote at a spot where the\nremainder was not valid EDN, so the reader threw. That is a non-zero exit and\nwould have read as a successful demonstration, but it exercises the reader, not\nthe check — the same trap as a break that trips a different gate. The\ndemonstration above is a file that parses.\n\n## What was deliberately not done\n\n**The launchd plist was not installed.** `scripts/com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick.plist`\nexists and remains unloaded. Registering detectors is reversible; making them\nrun unattended on a schedule is an owner decision, and the SessionStart hook\nlabels it as one.\n\n**Is it now worth installing?** The registry has gone from 2 entries to 6, and\nthree of the four added are red today on real defects nothing else is watching —\na JVM dependency outside a frozen inventory, a host-import contract that has\ndrifted in two directions, and seven layer violations. The cost is bounded and\nnow measured: at 6h intervals `verify-layer-deps` + `host-parity` + `cutover`\ntogether are under 3 minutes of wall time per tick, `verify-vendored-copies` is\n1m41s, and the two 24h entries are the expensive ones — `verify-require-graph`\nat 8m and `verify-tsukuru-factory-plane` at up to 18m under load. Worst case is\nroughly **30 minutes of mostly-waiting work per day** on a machine that already\nsustains load 150.\n\nThe argument against installing is not cost, it is that **ten findings are\nstanding red right now**. Installing the schedule before those are triaged\nproduces a report whose every line is STANDING from day one — the exact\ncondition ADR-2608124800 names as indistinguishable from silence. The honest\nrecommendation is: **install it, but only after a first pass over the ten**, so\nthat the first scheduled report has something to say other than what is already\nknown.\n", :adr/date "2026-08-13", :db/id -1, :adr/title "Eleven verify-* scripts swept: four registered in the orgs-detector tick, nine ruled out by stated class"}]
